  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe Erd\u0151s discrepancy problem requires minimizing the maximum overlap between a step function `h` and its complement shifted across `[0,2]`. This non-convex optimization problem is addressed using a genetic algorithm (GA) that evolves candidate solutions across multiple resolutions (n_points). The core insight is that the parent solution at n_points=45 has overlapping features that can be improved by adaptive repair and representation changes. The GA maintains population diversity via tournament selection, recombines favorable patterns with similarity-based crossover, and introduces intelligent mutations through Gaussian perturbations. The key innovation is a dynamic dimensional repair mechanism that scales solutions consistently across resolutions, ensuring the integral constraint remains satisfied. This method escapes local optima through adaptive inheritance from successful parents and iterative resolution refinement.\n\nMathematical specification:\n- Representation: Real-valued vectors `h` with entries `[0,1]` and `\u2211h = n_points/2`\n- Objective: Minimize `C5 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx = max(np.correlate(h,\u2227h,mode='full'))*(2/n_points)`\n- Constraints: \n  - `0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1`\n  - `\u2211(h) = n_points/2`\n- Operators:\n  - Crossover: Uniform crossover with uniform padding\n  - Mutation: Gaussian noise applied to selected elements\n  - Repair: Scaling by available room (1-h[i] and h[i])\n- Dimensional Handling: Linear interpolation between 45\u219290\u2192180 points\n- Repair Function: \n  `room_inc = 1 - h; room_dec = h`\n  `new_val \u2190 h_clip + [room_inc * (adj/sum(room_inc))]` (if sum adjustment needed)\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Validate `initial_h_values` (45 points), repair via linear scaling\n2. Initialize population (50 individuals) from parent and random samples\n3. For each generation (`elapsed time < budget`):\n   a. Tournament selection (3 candidates)\n   b. Crossover: Uniform padding (padded length 2*n_points)\n   c. Mutation: 15% chance per element, Gaussian noise \u03c3=0.1\n   d. Repair: Adjust sum proportionally by available room\n   e. Clamp values to [0,1] and re-scale integral\n   f. Evaluate top candidates, update best solution\n4. Maintain record of highest score improvement\n5. Return best solution and its C5 score\n\nSearch choices and useful variations:\n- Start n_points: 45\u219290\u2192180\n- Crossover rate: 70% population\n- Mutation rate: 15% genome\n- Initial population: Mutations of parent (40%) + random samples (60%)\n- Repair methods: Targeted scaling (default) + clipping fallback\n- Tournament size: All-dominant (minimum fitness)\n- Population size: Dynamic floor(25 + n_points/20)\n\nNumerical robustness and memory use:\n- Repair thresholds \u03b5=1e-5 for sum matching\n- Fallback: Integer repair if floating-point repair fails\n- Memory: (approx) 50 * (current n_points) floats\n- Handling n_points change: Interpolate parent between existing points\n- Convergence: Max 1000 generations (est. 60s per phase)\n\nRuntime safeguards and return contract:\n- Time logging: Start/stop per generation\n- Termination: Timeout or no improvement in minimal C5\n- Return format: (valid_h, C5_score, n_points)\n- Candidates: Ensure all entries `0<=h[i]<=1`\n- Fallback: Integer-repaired solution if repair fails\n- Main objective: Direct computation via np.correlate",
